      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates, we need to determine \\(\\rho\\), \\(\\theta\\), and \\(\\phi\\).\n\n**Step 1: Calculate \\(\\rho\\)**\n\nThe radial distance \\(\\rho\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\]\nSubstituting the coordinates \\((x, y, z) = (0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\):\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate \\(\\theta\\)**\n\nThe azimuthal angle \\(\\theta\\) is the angle in the \\(xy\\)-plane from the positive \\(x\\)-axis. Since \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\), the point lies on the negative \\(y\\)-axis. Therefore, \\(\\theta\\) is:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\pi\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate \\(\\phi\\)**\n\nThe polar angle \\(\\phi\\) is the angle from the positive \\(z\\)-axis to the point. We use the formula:\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{\\rho}\\right)\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(\\rho = 6\\):\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\n\\]\nSince \\(\\cos^{-1}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\phi = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(6, \\pi, \\frac{5\\pi}{6})}\n\\]",
