      "text": "To solve, we need to find the spherical coordinates of a point given in rectangular coordinates. The spherical coordinates are given by $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$, where $\\rho$ represents the distance from the origin, $\\theta$ represents the angle from the positive x-axis, and $\\phi$ represents the angle from the z-axis. \n\nFirst, we can calculate $\\rho$, the distance from the origin:\n$$\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6.$$\n\nNext, we find $\\theta$ (the angle from the positive x-axis):\n$$\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right).$$\n\nSince the x-coordinate is 0 and the y-coordinate is negative, $\\theta$ will be $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\phi$ (the angle from the z-axis):\n$$\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}.$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates are $\\boxed{(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}.$",
