      "text": "To solve the problem of converting the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. \\(\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\\)\n2. \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\) (considering the quadrant in the \\(xy\\)-plane)\n3. \\(\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\\) (considering the position of the point on the sphere and the poles)\n\nGiven the rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z) = (0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\):\n\n1. Calculate \\(\\rho\\):\n   \\[\n   \\rho = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(\\theta\\):\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n   \\]\n   This expression is undefined, so \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\) (since the point is in the opposite side of the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n3. Calculate \\(\\phi\\):\n   \\[\n   \\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   This gives \\(\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nPutting it all together, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}\n\\]",
