      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem of converting the rectangular coordinates \\((0, -3 \\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine \\(\\rho\\):**\n   The radial distance \\(\\rho\\) in spherical coordinates is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(x = 0\\), \\(y = -3 \\sqrt{3}\\), and \\(z = 3\\). Plugging these values in, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\rho = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3 \\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine \\(\\theta\\):**\n   The azimuthal angle \\(\\theta\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Since \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = -3 \\sqrt{3}\\), the point lies on the negative \\(y\\)-axis. Therefore, \\(\\theta\\) should be in the range \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{2} < \\theta < \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). However, the correct sign for \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\pi\\) because it points in the negative \\(y\\)-direction from the positive \\(x\\)-axis. Hence, \\(\\theta = \\pi\\).\n\n3. **Determine \\(\\phi\\):**\n   The polar angle \\(\\phi\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Substituting the values of \\(z\\) and \\(\\rho\\):\n   \\[\n   \\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   The angle \\(\\phi\\) is between \\(0\\) and \\(\\pi\\) (since \\(\\rho > 0\\) and \\(z \\geq 0\\)). The value of \\(\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) or \\(\\frac{\\pi}{180}\\) degrees, but we will use the spherical angle convention.\n\nPutting it all together, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(6, \\pi, \\frac{1}{2}) or (6, \\frac{3}{2}\\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}\n\\]\n\nHowever, due to the constant value of spherical coordinates, the answer in the form \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\) where \\(\\rho > 0\\), \\(0 \\le \\theta < 2 \\pi\\), and \\(0 \\le \\phi \\le \\pi\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(6, \\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}\n\\]",
